{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1350 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "20059e1497b4b2daddec6f138e6e5fa8654b2324fe6bafc673cdc400f7e680a1" :edb/db-before-t 1349 :edb/db-after-t 1350 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534662 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:36:26.169Z" 13194139534662 true] [17592186059586 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302126 13194139534662 true] [17592186059677 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302145 13194139534662 true] [17592186068716 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302138 13194139534662 true] [17592186078937 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534662 true] [17592186078937 1100 "The population $p$, in millions, of a particular colony of bacteria after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $p(t) = \\frac{2^{t^{2} + 1}}{\\ln 2}$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the bacteria population as a function of $t$?" 13194139534662 false] [17592186078937 1100 "The population $p$, in millions, of a particular colony of bacteria after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $p(t)=\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the bacteria population as a function of $t?$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186078937 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the bacteria population is equal to ${p}^{′}(t).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot (2t)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2t\\cdot {2}^{{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186083192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302131 13194139534662 true] [17592186087773 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302102 13194139534662 true] [17592186105736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302109 13194139534662 true] [17592186110592 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302116 13194139534662 true] [17592186179715 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302123 13194139534662 true] [17592186302102 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302103 13194139534662 true] [17592186302102 1089 "q-115309" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302102 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534662 true] [17592186302102 1100 "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36}=1?$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302102 1113 #uuid "cf51bfe3-04e9-56d5-87f7-d329556cf2cb" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302102 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=6,$ so the center is located at $(-4,6).$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302104 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302105 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302106 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302107 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302108 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1041 "selection" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302107 13194139534662 true] [17592186302103 1110 #uuid "739f30a4-1ba2-54f5-832e-1999e6e2dca8" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302104 1034 "(4,-6)" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302104 1111 #uuid "5bc9eb5d-a79f-5714-8233-7358713503ce" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302105 1034 "(2,6)" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302105 1111 #uuid "e1b82bf7-0d35-545c-b325-3b5470b9722a" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302106 1034 "(6,-4)" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302106 1111 #uuid "89497849-fd9d-5245-9d3a-4059493c2953" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302107 1034 "(-4,6)" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302107 1111 #uuid "2e288629-c875-57a7-a744-8beef0f21c22" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302108 1034 "(6,-2)" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302108 1111 #uuid "ec32dc70-8e70-5c28-ab7c-9d9d3d72a88e" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302109 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302110 13194139534662 true] [17592186302109 1089 "q-115699" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302109 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534662 true] [17592186302109 1100 "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *underestimate* of the area?" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302109 1113 #uuid "73a107f3-de5c-51c5-9778-c2302ba770ac" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302109 1145 "The right Riemann sum underestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is decreasing over $x\\in [-4,-1]$ is as follows.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038872/assets/8361d4cb197686b79cf21cb88ee70de5b3578e92fb758fb84aec754871dfca3e.png)" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302111 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302112 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302113 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302114 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302115 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1041 "selection" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302111 13194139534662 true] [17592186302110 1110 #uuid "39fe786b-4764-590d-b275-6412c661af10" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302111 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038872/assets/8361d4cb197686b79cf21cb88ee70de5b3578e92fb758fb84aec754871dfca3e.png" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534662 true] [17592186302111 1111 #uuid "ecf80c87-eef4-5f26-8b63-cbfb70bc33fc" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302112 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038872/assets/7c79516ab4216ae1eed208c87204ae045b7f070f13d98853d6fed56eaae9b1c6.png" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302112 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534662 true] [17592186302112 1111 #uuid "1075aa64-2813-5140-ba13-08a19d1f3025" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302113 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038872/assets/a20867cc6aca9f9b9a5b2b5780597938f43547380a6a0294671660f4a982383a.png" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302113 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534662 true] [17592186302113 1111 #uuid "9fd4dbb8-3ef1-5a11-847e-f240aa92d0c1" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302114 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038872/assets/9e57c21db9ca295e246eee4510497763c857d280338aba261a3f1c6fb309da9d.png" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534662 true] [17592186302114 1111 #uuid "37db84f6-1a5d-5bc8-ac1c-17f991eded16" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302115 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038872/assets/a4dc3b8db26fe963aca60dab6fd927581896329e1801c11ce2687be251d65840.png" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302115 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534662 true] [17592186302115 1111 #uuid "d857a929-20db-5181-ba45-3ff8acef0308" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302116 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302117 13194139534662 true] [17592186302116 1089 "q-142835" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302116 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534662 true] [17592186302116 1100 "$\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}=$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302116 1113 #uuid "d7b1e141-0f1f-5182-b6a5-be50ff0bb0dc" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302116 1145 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\,{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{2\\,(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2{tan}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}=1.\n$$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302118 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302119 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302120 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302121 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302122 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1041 "selection" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302120 13194139534662 true] [17592186302117 1110 #uuid "d308c519-f561-5b02-8c0f-0dd415d40d2c" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302118 1034 "1+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302118 1111 #uuid "d211f02d-c40f-50c2-a20d-deb0a43d3209" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302119 1034 "2" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302119 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302119 1111 #uuid "35ed506a-9521-5180-a96f-682b0dd8d833" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302120 1034 "1" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302120 1111 #uuid "bbd030d7-e3f3-5d11-a24e-1a39bd081e7e" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302121 1034 "1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302121 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302121 1111 #uuid "a85045ce-0e2b-583c-bb65-24f96d61b1b3" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302122 1034 "-1" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302122 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302122 1111 #uuid "3b7551bf-7805-5d9d-8ec0-f2ca0e1720cc" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302123 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302124 13194139534662 true] [17592186302123 1089 "q-242909" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302123 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534662 true] [17592186302123 1100 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x}={9}^{5-x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302123 1113 #uuid "b74609cf-54a2-5e61-b71b-f371e006d3b3" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302123 1145 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & ={9}^{5-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{5-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{5-x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx=(5-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(5-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302124 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302125 13194139534662 true] [17592186302124 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534662 true] [17592186302124 1041 "field-1" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302124 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302125 13194139534662 true] [17592186302124 1110 #uuid "98ddf865-1c62-526a-98a9-cb3cdff75bd0" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302125 1034 "\\frac{5\\ln(9)}{1+\\ln(9)}" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302125 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302125 1111 #uuid "186858b9-b9f0-5d60-9d72-9f09ea351310" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302126 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302127 13194139534662 true] [17592186302126 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302129 13194139534662 true] [17592186302126 1089 "q-332175" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302126 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534662 true] [17592186302126 1100 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{210}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{210}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302126 1113 #uuid "a4183e51-f524-53bb-8633-07760d485b70" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302126 1145 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={210}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{210}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{210}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302128 13194139534662 true] [17592186302127 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534662 true] [17592186302127 1041 "field-1" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302127 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302128 13194139534662 true] [17592186302127 1110 #uuid "57d18ad5-433f-50c0-afb7-21df4c1a2a0d" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302128 1034 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302128 1111 #uuid "10692e21-def1-58d9-82d7-41df447ada3d" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302129 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302130 13194139534662 true] [17592186302129 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534662 true] [17592186302129 1041 "field-2" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302129 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302130 13194139534662 true] [17592186302129 1110 #uuid "a52966d4-1d56-5452-9e0d-b3caebfa598f" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302130 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302130 1111 #uuid "e2326f7f-0b72-5413-ab31-73bd5817bdef" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302131 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302132 13194139534662 true] [17592186302131 1089 "q-40178" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302131 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534662 true] [17592186302131 1100 "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a fourth term equal to $24$ and a seventh term equal to $\\frac{3}{8}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302131 1113 #uuid "bf253c8e-f56d-577e-8af9-45821f9cd76e" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302131 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{4}=24$ and ${a}_{7}=\\frac{3}{8}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{24} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{8})(\\frac{1}{24}) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=4$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}(r)^{3} \\\\ 24 & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\ 24 & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{64}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1\\,536\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,536}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,536}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =1\\,536(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\,048.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302133 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302134 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302135 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302136 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302137 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1041 "selection" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302134 13194139534662 true] [17592186302132 1110 #uuid "ff1ac375-bbc8-5535-9f4c-59e4dde8a361" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302133 1034 "6\\,144" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302133 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302133 1111 #uuid "9e7d75ec-b6e8-5935-8161-cef7ff455977" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302134 1034 "2\\,048" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302134 1111 #uuid "66289b0e-3bc4-5cf9-a7af-30d98407fbf4" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302135 1034 "1\\,024" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302135 1111 #uuid "7ef8a7c3-11dd-559d-8f3c-9984fba7e000" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302136 1034 "3\\,072" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302136 1111 #uuid "a5bf168e-09f1-53ca-9703-3c0cd88098f7" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302137 1034 "4\\,364" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302137 1111 #uuid "464bf8ba-856d-5217-acc8-9f5419529fba" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302138 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302139 13194139534662 true] [17592186302138 1089 "q-54161" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302138 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534662 true] [17592186302138 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-1,\\,y=4{t}^{5}+1$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302138 1113 #uuid "fb260a57-62db-5317-884e-db3ce39bc673" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302138 1145 "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{20{t}^{4}}{2t}=10{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(10{t}^{3})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{30{t}^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =15t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302140 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302141 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302142 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302143 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302144 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1041 "selection" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302140 13194139534662 true] [17592186302139 1110 #uuid "7c8482ab-5b8e-5338-b0b8-f6e49c1b7e77" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302140 1034 "15t" 13194139534662 true] [17592186302140 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534662 true] [17592186302140 1111 #uuid "1d26